d
> with SE, and tense markers (various selma'o), which can't. Then there are=
 some
> words that look like prepositions, but are different:
> *FA puts a sumti in a numbered case slot. It is normally omitted if the s=
umti
> would be in that slot without it. A Lojban grammarian describing English =
would
> put "to", when used to mark the indirect object, in this class.
> *"pe" looks like a possessive preposition, but it's in the same selma'o a=
s
> "goi", which is not a preposition.
> *"be" looks like a preposition in phrases like "lo mamta be le verba", bu=
t it
> does something completely different.
